36:   1. merge that head in memory into local serialized metadata\nL37:   2. resolve merge conflicts according to value-type semantics\nL38:   3. update the local SQLite database with newly visible values and tombstones\nL39:   4. record when materialization succeeded\nL40: \nL41: Per-type conflict rules are defined in:\nL42: \nL43:   * cite4†Strings L44:   * cite5†Lists L45:   * cite6†Sets L46: \nL47: ## High-level scenarios\nL48: ### 1. Initial sync\nL49: \nL50: If the local system has no metadata yet:\nL51: \nL52:   * walk the tree at the incoming metadata head\nL53:   * materialize all visible values into SQLite\nL54:   * record that metadata commit as materialized\nL55: \nL56: No metadata history walk is required beyond the current tree.\nL57: ### 2. Multiple start points\nL58: \nL59: If local metadata exists and someone else already pushed an independently initialized metadata history:\nL60: \nL61:   * serialize local shareable state\nL62:   * perform a baseless two-way merge with the remote tree\nL63:   * for overlapping keys, remote wins\nL64:   * write a new metadata commit with the remote head as parent\nL65:   * retry if the remote advanced before push completed\nL66: \nL67: This allows eventual convergence while keeping history linear.\nL68: ### 3. Fast-forward update\nL69: \nL70: If the incoming metadata ref is a fast-forward from the last materialized point:\nL71: \nL72:   * diff the old materialized tree against the new tree\nL73:   * apply only changed key/values to SQLite\nL74:   * add or update visible values and tombstones\nL75: ### 4. Both sides mutated data\nL76: \nL77: If local data changed and remote data also changed:\nL78: \nL79:   * serialize local current shareable state\nL80:   * merge the remote tree into it\nL81:   * resolve conflicts according to per-type semantics\nL82:   * write a new metadata commit from the merged tree\nL83: \nL84: On push, if it is again not a fast-forward, do this sequence repeatedly against newer remote heads until a fast-forward push succeeds. Only one new commit locally needs to be made.\nL85: ## No common ancestor merge\nL86: \nL87: If local and remote metadata histories have no common ancestor, materialize uses a two-way merge instead of a three-way merge.\nL88: \nL89:   1. union non-conflicting keys from both sides\nL90:   2. for overlapping keys or value-vs-tombstone conflicts, remote wins\nL91:   3. retain non-overlapping keys from both sides\nL92: ## Removal handling\nL93: \nL94: Deletion is explicit.\nL95: \nL96: During materialization:\nL97: \nL98:   * whole-key tombstones remove keys locally\nL99:   * per-entry or per-member tombstones are applied according to the collection type\nL100: ## Metadata history shape\nL101: \nL102: Unlike source-code history, metadata history does not need rich branch/merge topology.\nL103: \nL104: The preferred shape is linear history created by repeatedly:\nL105: \nL106:   * merging in memory\nL107:   * writing a new commit\nL108:   * fast-forward pushing\nL109: \nL110: The main goal is convergence on current metadata state, not preserving meaningful branch structure.\nL111: ## Merging\nL112: \nL113:   * update locally, pruned remotely?\nL114: \nL115: On this page\nL116: \nL117:   * cite20†Materialize responsibilities L118:   * cite21†High-level scenarios L119:     * cite22†1. Initial sync L120:     * cite23†2. Multiple start points L121:     * cite24†3. Fast-forward update L122:     * cite25†4. Both sides mutated data L123:   * cite26†No common ancestor merge L124:   * cite27†Removal handling L125:   * cite28†Metadata history shape L126:   * cite29†Merging --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrings · git-meta (https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/strings.html)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/strings.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 80\nL0: cite0†git-meta L1: \nL2: Git metadata specification and project docs\nL3: \nL4: cite0†Overview L5: \nL6: ## Exchange format\nL7: \nL8: cite1†Targets and keys cite2†Serialization cite3†Materialization L9: \nL10: ## Value types\nL11: \nL12: cite4†Strings cite5†Lists cite6†Sets L13: \nL14: ## Implementation\nL15: \nL16: cite7†Implementation Overview cite8†Rust Library API cite9†Local storage cite10†CLI surface cite11†Output and query semantics cite12†Auto-pruning cite13†Serialize filters L17: ## Other Considerations\nL18: \nL19: cite14†Standard keys cite15†Remote Management cite16†Syncing with normal Git cite17†Workflow L20: \nL21: ☀️ 🌙\nL22: \nL23: ◧\nL24: \nL25: Generated from cite18†`spec/*.md`†github.com L26: \nL27: # Strings\nL28: \nL29: cite19†{ } markdown†raw.githubusercontent.com L30: \nL31: Strings are the simplest git-meta value type.\nL32: \nL33: A string represents one current scalar value for a `(target, key)` pair.\nL34: \nL35: Examples:\nL36: \nL37:   * `agent:model = claude-4.6`\nL38:   * `owner = schacon`\nL39:   * `review:signoff = schacon@gmail.com`\nL40: ## Summary\nL41: \nL42:   * One current value per key\nL43:   * Serialized as a single terminal blob\nL44:   * Removal uses a key tombstone\nL45:   * Concurrent updates resolve by last-writer-wins\nL46: \nL47: ## Serialized tree shape\nL48: \nL49: For a string value, write the string to:\nL50: \nL51: `[base]/<key segments>/__value`\nL52: \nL53: Examples:\nL54: \nL55:   * `commit/13/<full-target>/agent/model/__value`\nL56:   * `path/src/metrics/__target__/owner/__value`\nL57: \nL58: The blob contents are the raw string value.\nL59: ## Tombstones\nL60: \nL61: If a string key is removed, serialize a tombstone at:\nL62: \nL63: `[base]/__tombstones/<key segments>/__deleted`\nL64: \nL65: The tombstone blob should be the same blob as the `__value` was. This way Git reuses the blob and we can do content comparisons if needed in cases of conflict.\nL66: \nL67: When a string is set again after deletion, the tombstone is cleared locally and omitted from the next serialization.\nL68: ## Merge semantics\nL69: \nL70: Merging trees can technically use either `ours` or `theirs` strategies, but one value should win out. Projects or individuals can determine which they prefer.\nL71: \nL72: The default in our implementation is `ours`, so that both values are in the history and reverting is possible.\nL73: \nL74: On this page\nL75: \nL76:   * cite20†Summary L77:   * cite21†Serialized tree shape L78:   * cite22†Tombstones L79:   * cite23†Merge semantics --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLists · git-meta (https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/lists.html)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://git-meta.com/spec/exchange-format/lists.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 104\nL0: cite0†git-meta L1: \nL2: Git metadata specification and project docs\nL3: \nL4: cite0†Overview L5: \nL6: ## Exchange format\nL7: \nL8: cite1†Targets and keys cite2†Serialization cite3†Materialization L9: \nL10: ## Value types\nL11: \nL12: cite4†Strings cite5†Lists cite6†Sets L13: \nL14: ## Implementation\nL15: \nL16: cite7†Implementation Overview cite8†Rust Library API cite9†Local storage cite10†CLI surface cite11†Output and query semantics cite12†Auto-pruning cite13†Serialize filters L17: ## Other Considerations\nL18: \nL19: cite14†Standard keys cite15†Remote Management cite16†Syncing with normal Git cite17†Workflow L20: \nL21: ☀️ 🌙\nL22: \nL23: ◧\nL24: \nL25: Generated from cite18†`spec/*.md`†github.com L26: \nL27: # Lists\nL28: \nL29: cite19†{ } markdown†raw.githubusercontent.com L30: \nL31: Lists in git-meta are append-friendly multi-value collections.\nL32: \nL33: They are best thought of as an ordered append log of string entries, not as a general-purpose mutable array.\nL34: \nL35: Examples:\nL36: \nL37:   * agent transcript jsonl chunks\nL38:   * review comments\nL39:   * a sequence of notes appended over time\nL40: ## Intended semantics\nL41: \nL42: The current list design is optimized for the most common concurrent operation: one or more users append new values to the same logical list.\nL43: \nL44: We serialize lists as many small blobs rather than one JSON array blob so we can use normal Git tree merging tooling to avoid array-level conflicts.\nL45: ## Serialized tree shape\nL46: \nL47: For a list value, entries are written under:\nL48: \nL49: `[base]/<key segments>/__list/<entry-id>`\nL50: \nL51: Where `<entry-id>` is of the form:\nL52: \nL53: `<timestamp-ms>-<content-hash-prefix>`\nL54: \nL55: Examples:\nL56: \nL57:   * `branch/06/sc-branch-1-deadbeef/agent/chat/__list/1771232450203-23c0f`\nL58:   * `path/src/metrics/__target__/comments/__list/1771232450203-23c0f`\nL59: \nL60: Each blob contains one string entry.\nL61: If multiple values are inserted in one command, start from one base millisecond timestamp and increment by 1 for each additional entry so local order is preserved.\nL62: ## Ordering\nL63: \nL64: List order is defined by sorting `<entry-id>` lexically, which is equivalent to sorting by:\nL65: \nL66:   1. timestamp ascending\nL67:   2. hash suffix as a stable tie-breaker\nL68: \nL69: This gives deterministic ordering after merges.\nL70: \nL71: ## Duplicate values\nL72: \nL73: Lists allow duplicate string values.\nL74: \nL75: If the same text is appended twice at different times, both entries remain.\nL76: \nL77: This is intentional and differentiates lists from sets.\nL78: \nL79: ## Tombstones\nL80: ### Whole-key removal\nL81: \nL82: If the entire list key is removed, write a tombstone at:\nL83: \nL84: `[base]/__tombstones/<key segments>/__deleted`\nL85: \nL86: This means the logical list key has been removed.\nL87: ### Entry removal\nL88: \nL89: Removing individual entries in a list after the fact can be done by adding a tombstone to individual entry ids.\nL90: \nL91: `[base]/<key segments>/__tombstones/<entry-id>/__deleted`\nL92: \nL93: Where the `__deleted` blob is the original contents of the entry.\nL94: Because `__tombstones` is shared across collection types, serialize and materialize must ignore incompatible child tombstones for the current key type. For example, if a key currently materializes as a list, only tombstones that are valid list entry identifiers should affect the visible list state; incompatible child tombstones should be preserved but ignored.\nL95: \nL96: On this page\nL97:   * cite20†Intended semantics L98:   * cite21†Serialized tree shape L99:   * cite22†Ordering L100:   * cite23†Duplicate values L101:   * cite24†Tombstones L102:     * cite25†Whole-key removal L103:     * cite26†Entry removal ","type":"input_text"}],"session_id":"01a08fab-6619-7640-b60b-6c08aa561526","source_line":1891,"timestamp":"2026-09-11T11:21:41.091Z","type":"custom_tool_call_output"}
